## What it is

`async`/`await` is a **compiler** feature, not a runtime one. The C# compiler rewrites an `async` body into a state machine; each `await` is a point where that machine may suspend and resume. The CLR sees an ordinary method that fills a struct, calls `Start` on a *method builder* and returns whatever the builder exposes: `Task`, `Task<T>`, `ValueTask<T>`, or any type carrying `[AsyncMethodBuilder]` (C# 7).

## How it works underneath

For `async Task<int> M()` the compiler emits a nested struct (`<M>d__0`; a class in Debug builds so the debugger can inspect it) implementing `IAsyncStateMachine`, with:

- `int <>1__state`: `-1` running/not started, `-2` finished, `n >= 0` suspended at await number `n`;
- `AsyncTaskMethodBuilder<int> <>t__builder`, which creates and completes the returned task;
- hoisted parameters and locals that must survive across an `await`;
- one awaiter field per awaiter type (`TaskAwaiter<int> <>u__1`).

Calling `M()` runs `MoveNext()` **synchronously on the caller's thread**. At each `await e` the generated code does `awaiter = e.GetAwaiter(); if (awaiter.IsCompleted) { take the result and keep going } else { state = n; builder.AwaitUnsafeOnCompleted(ref awaiter, ref this); return; }`.

Two things happen on that first real suspension. First, the builder boxes the struct — since .NET Core 2.1 the box is an `AsyncStateMachineBox<TStateMachine>` that *is* the `Task` object: one allocation instead of at least four on .NET Framework (boxed state machine, `MoveNextRunner`, `Action` delegate, `Task`). Second, the awaiter's `UnsafeOnCompleted` registers `MoveNext` as the continuation of the awaited task. `TaskAwaiter` captures `SynchronizationContext.Current` (or a non-default `TaskScheduler.Current`) unless the awaitable came from `ConfigureAwait(false)`; the builder separately captures `ExecutionContext`, so `AsyncLocal<T>` values flow regardless of `ConfigureAwait`.

When the awaited task completes, the continuation either runs inline on the thread that completed it — unless that thread sits on a non-default `SynchronizationContext`/`TaskScheduler`, in which case the runtime queues it to the thread pool rather than inlining (`TaskCreationOptions.RunContinuationsAsynchronously` opts out of inlining altogether) — or is posted to the captured context. `MoveNext` re-enters at `case n`, calls `awaiter.GetResult()` — which rethrows the original exception, not an `AggregateException` — and continues. `return x` becomes `builder.SetResult(x)`; anything thrown from the body is caught inside `MoveNext` and becomes `builder.SetException(ex)` (an `OperationCanceledException` makes the task `Canceled`). So an `async Task` method never throws synchronously, even for argument validation placed before the first `await`; eager throwing needs a non-`async` wrapper.

**Hot path.** If every awaiter reports `IsCompleted == true`, the struct never leaves the stack; the only allocation is the `Task<int>` itself, and `AsyncTaskMethodBuilder<T>` skips even that for cached results (`true`/`false`, `int` in `-1..8`, `null`). For everything else — `Task<string>`, a buffered `ReadAsync` returning 4096 — `ValueTask<T>` removes the allocation: it is a struct holding the result inline, or wrapping a `Task<T>`, or (since .NET Core 2.1) an `IValueTaskSource<T>` that `Socket`, `PipeReader` and `ChannelReader<T>` implement with pooled, reusable objects. Since .NET 6 / C# 10 the box for an `async ValueTask<T>` method itself can be pooled via `[AsyncMethodBuilder(typeof(PoolingAsyncValueTaskMethodBuilder<>))]` on the method. The price is a stricter contract: await a `ValueTask` **once**, never call `.Result` before it completed, never await it concurrently, and call `AsTask()` (or `Preserve()`) *instead of* that single await when you need anything else — a pooled source is recycled right after the first `GetResult`.

## What an interviewer probes

- Which thread runs the code before the first `await`? The caller's; no thread is created.
- What does `ConfigureAwait(false)` really change? Only whether the awaiter captures `SynchronizationContext`/`TaskScheduler`. It does nothing when the task is already complete (no continuation is scheduled), and it does not stop `ExecutionContext` flow. ASP.NET Core has no `SynchronizationContext`, so there it is a micro-optimisation; it stays mandatory in libraries because the same code may run under WPF, WinForms, MAUI or Blazor, which do have one. .NET 8 added `ConfigureAwait(ConfigureAwaitOptions)`: `ForceYielding` (always suspend, like `Task.Yield()`), `SuppressThrowing` (non-generic `Task` only; `Task<T>` throws `ArgumentOutOfRangeException`) and `ContinueOnCapturedContext`.
- `Task` vs `ValueTask` in a public API? Default to `Task` (awaitable by many consumers, cacheable, `WhenAll`-friendly); choose `ValueTask` when synchronous completion dominates or a pooled `IValueTaskSource` backs it.
- What is next? .NET 10 ships an experimental, opt-in *runtime async* mode where the JIT implements suspension natively instead of compiler-emitted state machines.

## Common misconceptions

- "`async` runs the method on another thread" — it runs synchronously until the first incomplete awaiter.
- "`ConfigureAwait(false)` moves the continuation to the thread pool" — it only removes the capture.
- "`ValueTask` is a cheaper `Task`" — it is a different, single-consumption contract.

