## What it is

A `Thread` is an OS-level execution unit: a kernel object with its own stack (about 1 MB reserved by default; .NET uses 1.5 MB on Linux), scheduled pre-emptively by the operating system. A `Task` is not a thread. It is a promise: a handle to work that may complete in the future, carrying its result, exception and completion callbacks. Some tasks are backed by a thread pool thread executing a delegate (`Task.Run`); many are not backed by any thread at all (a pending socket read, a `TaskCompletionSource`, a `Task.Delay` timer).

## How the thread pool works underneath

Since .NET 6 the worker pool is the *portable* (managed) thread pool in `System.Private.CoreLib`; .NET 7 moved Windows I/O completion handling into it as well, so from .NET 7 the implementation is the same on every OS. It has a global FIFO queue plus one local queue per worker thread. Work queued from a non-pool thread goes to the global queue; work queued *from a pool thread* (`Task.Run` inside a task, continuations, `Parallel` sub-ranges) lands in that worker's local queue. The owner pops its local queue LIFO (hot cache, likely-related work); idle workers *steal* from the opposite end (FIFO) of other workers' queues before falling back to the global queue. That is work stealing, and it is why fan-out from inside a task is cheap.

Thread count is governed by three mechanisms:

- **Minimum threads** default to `Environment.ProcessorCount`. Up to that number, a new worker is created immediately when a work item arrives and no thread is idle.
- **Starvation-driven injection**: above the minimum, if the queue is non-empty and no work item completed since the last check, the pool adds a thread, but only about one every 500 ms (roughly 1 to 2 threads per second). When some items still complete, starvation detection stays quiet and growth is left to hill climbing, which is equally slow (a few threads per second at best); either way a blocked pool recovers gradually rather than instantly.
- **Hill climbing**: a feedback controller that periodically varies the thread count, measures throughput (completions per interval) and moves toward the count that maximises it. It also retires threads when fewer produce the same throughput.

.NET 6 added *blocking-aware* injection: when a pool thread blocks on an incomplete `Task` (`.Wait()`, `.Result`, `GetAwaiter().GetResult()`) the pool notices and injects faster than the 500 ms cadence. Blocking on a `lock`, a synchronous socket or `Thread.Sleep` is invisible to it and still hits the slow path.

I/O-bound `await` uses none of this capacity while waiting: the request is handed to the OS (I/O completion ports on Windows, `epoll`/`kqueue` on Linux/macOS), the thread returns to the pool, and the completion enqueues the continuation as ordinary work.

## `Task.Run` in ASP.NET Core: why not

Every request already runs on a thread pool thread. `Task.Run(() => DoWork())` inside a request handler takes work off one pool thread and puts it on another: zero extra capacity, one extra queue hop and context switch, and `HttpContext` (not thread-safe, recycled at the end of the request) now touched from a thread that may outlive the response. Wrapping a *synchronous* call in `Task.Run` does not make it asynchronous; it just blocks a different pool thread. Only a truly asynchronous API awaited to completion frees a thread.

Legitimate patterns instead:

- I/O: `await` the async API (`ReadAsync`, `ToListAsync`, `SendAsync`).
- CPU-heavy work that must not stall a UI thread: `Task.Run` is right in desktop and Blazor Hybrid apps, where the caller is a single dedicated thread.
- Work that should outlive the request: a `Channel<T>` consumed by a `BackgroundService`, not a fire-and-forget `Task.Run` capturing scoped services.
- A long-lived loop (message pump, queue consumer): `TaskCreationOptions.LongRunning` or `new Thread`, so the pool's hill-climbing does not count it as a busy worker.

## What an interviewer probes

- "Does `await` create a thread?" (No. It releases one for I/O and reuses one for the continuation.)
- "Why did p99 latency spike for 30 seconds after a burst of blocking calls?" (Injection at ~2 threads/s above `ProcessorCount`.)
- "Why does `ThreadPool.SetMinThreads(500, 500)` mask the problem but not fix it?" (It only raises the fast-creation ceiling; the blocking is still there.)

## Common misconceptions

- "`Task.Run` starts a new thread." It queues to the pool; a thread is created only if the pool decides to grow.
- "More tasks means more parallelism." Parallelism is bounded by worker threads; more queued tasks only means a longer queue.
- "Wrapping a sync call in `Task.Run` makes an endpoint scalable." It moves the block; it does not remove it.

```csharp
// ReportDb, PdfRenderer, Job and PumpQueueForever are elided helpers.
// Request handler on ASP.NET Core: all three run on pool threads.
app.MapGet("/report/{id}", async (int id, ReportDb db, CancellationToken ct) =>
{
    // BAD: a pool thread is still blocked for the whole query; Task.Run only added a hop.
    // var rows = await Task.Run(() => db.LoadRowsSync(id));

    // GOOD: the socket read is handed to the OS; no thread is held while waiting.
    var rows = await db.LoadRowsAsync(id, ct);

    // ACCEPTABLE only if genuinely CPU-heavy; it still competes with other requests.
    var pdf = await Task.Run(() => PdfRenderer.Render(rows), ct);
    return Results.File(pdf, "application/pdf");
});

// Long-lived consumer: not pool work, so give it its own thread.
Channel<Job> channel = Channel.CreateBounded<Job>(1_000);
Task consumer = Task.Factory.StartNew(
    () => PumpQueueForever(channel.Reader),
    CancellationToken.None,
    TaskCreationOptions.LongRunning,
    TaskScheduler.Default);
```

