## What they are

.NET ships a small toolbox of synchronization primitives, and a senior candidate is expected to know which one fits which shape of problem, not just that `lock` exists.

- **`lock` / `Monitor`** — the classic mutual-exclusion lock. `lock (obj) { ... }` compiles to `Monitor.Enter(obj, ref lockTaken)` plus `Monitor.Exit` in a `finally`. It is **reentrant** (the owning thread can re-enter), **thread-affine** (only the thread that entered may exit), and it **blocks the thread**. `Monitor.Wait`/`Pulse` turn it into a condition variable; `Monitor.TryEnter` adds timeouts. Because a continuation after `await` may resume on a different thread, the compiler refuses `await` inside a `lock` body (error CS1996).
- **`System.Threading.Lock` (C# 13 / .NET 9)** — a dedicated lock type. When the locked expression is statically typed as `Lock`, `lock (x)` compiles to `using (x.EnterScope()) { ... }` — a `ref struct` scope, no `Monitor`, no object-header sync block. It is still reentrant, still thread-affine, still not awaitable. Beware of casting a `Lock` to `object`: `lock ((object)myLock)` silently falls back to `Monitor` on the same object and does **not** synchronize with `EnterScope` callers (the compiler warns with CS9216 when it can see the conversion).
- **`SemaphoreSlim`** — a counting semaphore with `Wait` **and** `WaitAsync`. It has no thread affinity: whoever holds a slot may `Release()` from any thread, which is exactly why `new SemaphoreSlim(1, 1)` is the idiomatic *async mutex*. It is **not reentrant** — a method that awaits `WaitAsync` and then calls itself will deadlock on itself. `Release()` past `maxCount` throws `SemaphoreFullException`.
- **`Interlocked`** — hardware atomic operations: `Increment`, `Add`, `Exchange`, `CompareExchange`, `Or`/`And` (.NET 5+). All `Interlocked` read-modify-write operations have full-fence semantics per the .NET memory model. `count++` is a read-modify-write and is not atomic even for `int`; `volatile` gives acquire/release ordering and visibility, not atomicity of a read-modify-write. `CompareExchange` in a loop is the building block of lock-free algorithms and of the "copy-on-write + swap an immutable snapshot" pattern. .NET 9 extended `Exchange`/`CompareExchange` to `byte`, `short`, `bool` and enum types.
- **`ReaderWriterLockSlim`** — many concurrent readers, one exclusive writer, plus an *upgradeable read* mode (only one holder at a time). Default `LockRecursionPolicy.NoRecursion` throws `LockRecursionException` on re-entry. Thread-affine, not awaitable, `IDisposable`. It only pays off when critical sections are long and read-mostly; for short sections a plain `lock` is usually faster because RWLS bookkeeping costs more than the contention it removes.
- **`Channel<T>`** (`System.Threading.Channels`) — an async producer/consumer queue. `Channel.CreateUnbounded<T>()` never blocks writers (memory can grow without limit); `Channel.CreateBounded<T>(capacity)` applies **backpressure**: `WriteAsync` awaits until a slot frees, `TryWrite` returns `false`, and `BoundedChannelFullMode` can instead `DropOldest`/`DropNewest`/`DropWrite`. `SingleReader`/`SingleWriter` options unlock faster implementations. `Writer.Complete()` lets `ReadAllAsync()` (an `IAsyncEnumerable<T>`) finish and `Reader.Completion` observe the terminal exception.

## What an interviewer probes

1. **Why can't I `await` inside `lock`?** Monitor ownership is per thread; after an `await` you may be on another thread, so `Exit` would throw `SynchronizationLockException`. The fix is `SemaphoreSlim.WaitAsync` — or restructure so the awaited I/O happens outside the critical section.
2. **Which of these are reentrant?** `Monitor`/`lock` and `Lock`: yes. `SemaphoreSlim`: no. `ReaderWriterLockSlim`: only with `SupportsRecursion`, which is discouraged.
3. **When is `Interlocked` enough?** A single word updated atomically (counters, flags, a reference to an immutable snapshot). The moment an invariant spans two fields you need a lock.
4. **Bounded or unbounded channel?** Bounded almost always in a server: an unbounded channel converts a slow consumer into an out-of-memory incident.

## Common misconceptions

- "`lock (this)` / `lock (typeof(T))` / `lock ("str")` are fine." They expose the lock object to code you do not control; interned strings are shared process-wide. Use a private `Lock` (or a private `readonly object`).
- "`SemaphoreSlim(1,1)` is just an async `lock`." It is not reentrant and it does not enforce that the releaser is the acquirer, so a missing `finally` leaks a slot forever.
- "`volatile` makes `++` thread-safe." It does not; use `Interlocked.Increment`.
- "`ReaderWriterLockSlim` is always faster for reads." Measure; for small sections `lock` or an `Interlocked`-swapped immutable snapshot typically wins.

## Sample: `Lock`, `SemaphoreSlim` and `Interlocked` together

```csharp
public sealed class TokenCache
{
    private readonly Lock _gate = new();                 // C# 13 / .NET 9
    private readonly SemaphoreSlim _refresh = new(1, 1); // async mutex, not reentrant
    private string? _token;
    private int _hits;

    public string? Peek()
    {
        Interlocked.Increment(ref _hits);                // atomic counter
        lock (_gate) { return _token; }                  // EnterScope, not Monitor
    }

    public async Task<string> GetAsync(CancellationToken ct)
    {
        await _refresh.WaitAsync(ct);                    // may resume on another thread
        try
        {
            string? cached;
            lock (_gate) { cached = _token; }            // every _token access goes through _gate
            if (cached is not null) return cached;

            var fetched = await FetchAsync(ct);          // awaiting inside lock(...) would be CS1996
            lock (_gate) { _token = fetched; }
            return fetched;
        }
        finally { _refresh.Release(); }                  // release from whichever thread we are on
    }

    private static Task<string> FetchAsync(CancellationToken ct) => Task.FromResult("tok");
}
```

